.TH FAN\-CONTROLLERD 8 "2026-10-04" "fan-controllerd 1.0.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
fan\-controllerd \- map hard\-drive temperatures to fan speed over USB serial
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fan\-controllerd
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-once ]
.br
.B fan\-controllerd
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fan\-controllerd
reads the temperature of each configured drive from the kernel
.B drivetemp
hwmon, takes the hottest, maps it through a piecewise\-linear curve, and sends
the resulting duty to an ESP32\-C3 fan controller over USB serial. It repeats
this every
.I poll_interval_s
seconds.
.PP
The controller keeps its own failsafe and drives the fan at full speed whenever
it stops hearing from the host, so stopping this daemon raises airflow rather
than losing it. For that to hold, the daemon refuses to start if the configured
poll interval plus the bounded serial exchange could exceed half the
controller's failsafe timeout. The firmware reports that timeout itself, and
when it differs from
.IR controller_failsafe_timeout_s ,
the daemon checks the poll interval against the real value each time it
connects, and exits if the interval no longer fits.
.PP
A drive whose temperature cannot be read is an error, not a missing sample: the
daemon commands 100%. There is no fallback temperature source, so the
.B drivetemp
module is a hard requirement. Drives behind a USB bridge and NVMe drives cannot
be monitored.
.PP
Each cycle also reads the controller's status before sending the new duty, and
checks it three ways: the duty being applied should be the one last commanded,
the failsafe should not have taken over since, and the tachometer should not be
below what that duty produces. Separately, the daemon reports any drive at or
above its own
.I temp1_max
or
.IR temp1_crit .
All of these are reported, not acted on: more duty cannot revive a dead fan,
and a hot drive already gets whatever the curve gives for its temperature.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Read configuration from
.IR path
instead of
.IR /etc/fan\-controllerd.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-once
Run a single measure\-and\-control cycle with verbose output, then exit. This is
not a dry run: it really commands the fan. Because the daemon then exits, the
controller's failsafe takes the fan to full speed once its timeout elapses. The
serial port is opened exclusively, so stop the service first.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit 0.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after
.BR \-\-help ,
.B \-\-version
or a completed
.B \-\-once
cycle; 2 on a usage error; 1 when the configuration is invalid, when the
controller's own failsafe timeout leaves no room for the configured poll
interval, or on any other failure that stops it. Otherwise it runs until
signalled.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter, in env_logger syntax. The default is
.BR info ,
or
.B debug
with
.BR \-\-once .
Under systemd every line carries its syslog priority, so
.B journalctl \-p warning \-u fan\-controllerd
shows only the problems.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/fan\-controllerd.toml
Configuration. Rendered with placeholder device and disk identifiers unless the
build was given real ones, and they must be right before the daemon is useful.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/fan\-controllerd/examples/
In the Debian package only: a copy of the annotated configuration as shipped, and an
optional udev rule that gives the controller a stable
.I /dev/fan\-controller
symlink.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR udevadm (8)
.PP
The serial and HTTP interfaces are specified in
.I PROTOCOL.md
at https://github.com/wxx9248/PC\-Fan\-Controller, which a Debian package built
from the full repository also installs under
.IR /usr/share/doc/fan\-controllerd/ .
